{"confidence": "secondary-source", "content": "## Atomic summary\n\nAccessing a GPU over a network and scheduling independent jobs across authenticated Nivus nodes are different architectures. A network-attached GPU demo does not show that multiple machines automatically pool RAM/VRAM or accelerate arbitrary model inference. Nivus task federation should first split independent jobs, declare node capacity, use leases and quotas, and validate checksummed outputs.\n\n## When it applies\n\nUse this distinction when evaluating WiCi-like demonstrations, P2P file sharing, remote inference servers or the future Nivus scheduler. Benchmark latency, bandwidth, compute and failure recovery for the specific task.\n\n## Evidence and limits\n\nThe linked NetworkChuck video is represented by secondary caption mirrors and promotional claims; the demo's performance has not been independently benchmarked here. The Nivus roadmap explicitly treats P2P and model parallelism as separate decisions requiring measurements.\n\n## References\n\n- [Video research index](../../docs/VIDEO_RESEARCH_INDEX.md)\n- [Nivus task federation roadmap](../../docs/PROJECT_ROADMAP.md)", "id": "networked-gpu-versus-task-federation", "record_type": "topic-note", "source": "docs/VIDEO_RESEARCH_INDEX.md; docs/PROJECT_ROADMAP.md", "source_file": "knowledge/topics/networked-gpu-vs-task-federation.md", "tags": ["wifi", "gpu", "network", "inference", "p2p", "node", "scheduler", "bandwidth", "latency", "lease"], "title": "Networked GPU access differs from Nivus task federation", "tokens": ["a", "accelerate", "access", "accessing", "across", "and", "applies", "arbitrary", "architectures", "are", "as", "atomic", "attached", "authenticated", "automatically", "bandwidth", "been", "benchmark", "benchmarked", "by", "capacity", "caption", "checksummed", "claims", "compute", "decisions", "declare", "demo", "demonstrations", "different", "differs", "distinction", "distributed", "docs", "does", "evaluating", "evidence", "explicitly", "failure", "federation", "file", "first", "for", "from", "future", "gpu", "has", "here", "independent", "independently", "index", "inference", "is", "it", "jobs", "latency", "lease", "leases", "like", "limits", "linked", "machines", "md", "measurements", "mirrors", "model", "multiple", "network", "networkchuck", "networked", "nivus", "node", "nodes", "not", "or", "outputs", "over", "p2p", "parallelism", "performance", "pool", "project_roadmap", "promotional", "quotas", "ram", "recovery", "references", "remote", "represented", "requiring", "research", "roadmap", "s", "scheduler", "scheduling", "secondary", "separate", "servers", "sharing", "should", "show", "specific", "split", "summary", "task", "that", "the", "this", "treats", "use", "validate", "video", "video_research_index", "vram", "when", "wici", "wifi"], "topic": "distributed-compute", "verified_on": "2026-11-15"}
{"confidence": "secondary-source", "content": "## Atomic summary\n\nA persistent development executor can accept tasks through an authorized chat/webhook, reserve work from a durable queue, execute in an isolated workspace, open a pull request and wait for CI and human review. A small always-on notebook may dispatch work while an ephemeral hosted runner handles code execution; the durable queue remains in GitHub. Supervision/restart, bounded retry, idempotency and persisted task state make it resilient to interruptions. This is a workflow pattern, not a guaranteed uninterrupted service.\n\n## When it applies\n\nUse it for asynchronous repository work when the operator may be offline. Keep it separate from the Nivus edge runtime and its telemetry. A notebook controller is optional; cross-node telemetry becomes useful when the Nivus Master is operational. Do not let the agent merge or deploy production changes.\n\n## Evidence and limits\n\nThe Hermes Docker documentation describes a persistent messaging gateway; Hermes, OmniRoute, Jira, Discord and GitHub capabilities are documented separately. The exact integration reported in the video comment has not been confirmed from a video transcript or a reproducible deployment.\n\n## References\n\n- [Nivus component analysis](../../docs/AI_DEVELOPMENT_ORCHESTRATION.md)\n- [Module work packages and integration plan](../../docs/AI_MODULE_WORKPLAN.md)\n- [Hermes persistent gateway documentation](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/docs/user-guide/docker)\n- [GitHub deployment reviews](https://docs.github.com/en/actions/how-tos/deploy/configure-and-manage-deployments/review-deployments)", "id": "ai-development-persistent-worker", "record_type": "topic-note", "source": "docs/AI_DEVELOPMENT_ORCHESTRATION.md; https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/docs/user-guide/docker", "source_file": "knowledge/topics/persistent-ai-development.md", "tags": ["hermes", "omniroute", "discord", "jira", "github", "queue", "worker", "pull-request", "ci", "human-review"], "title": "Persistent AI development worker with review gates", "tokens": ["a", "accept", "actions", "agent", "ai", "ai_development_orchestration", "ai_module_workplan", "always", "an", "analysis", "and", "applies", "are", "asynchronous", "atomic", "authorized", "be", "becomes", "been", "bounded", "can", "capabilities", "changes", "chat", "ci", "code", "com", "comment", "component", "configure", "confirmed", "controller", "cross", "deploy", "deployment", "deployments", "describes", "development", "discord", "dispatch", "do", "docker", "docs", "documentation", "documented", "durable", "edge", "en", "ephemeral", "evidence", "exact", "execute", "execution", "executor", "for", "from", "gates", "gateway", "github", "guaranteed", "guide", "handles", "has", "hermes", "hosted", "how", "https", "human", "idempotency", "in", "integration", "interruptions", "is", "isolated", "it", "its", "jira", "keep", "let", "limits", "make", "manage", "master", "may", "md", "merge", "messaging", "module", "nivus", "node", "not", "notebook", "nousresearch", "offline", "omniroute", "on", "open", "operational", "operator", "optional", "or", "orchestration", "packages", "pattern", "persisted", "persistent", "plan", "production", "pull", "queue", "references", "remains", "reported", "repository", "reproducible", "request", "reserve", "resilient", "restart", "retry", "review", "reviews", "runner", "runtime", "separate", "separately", "service", "small", "state", "summary", "supervision", "task", "tasks", "telemetry", "the", "this", "through", "to", "tos", "transcript", "uninterrupted", "use", "useful", "user", "video", "wait", "webhook", "when", "while", "with", "work", "worker", "workflow", "workspace"], "topic": "ai-development-orchestration", "verified_on": "2026-11-15"}
